.TH DARPA-SPILL-DUMP 1 "October 2026" "darpa_spillserver 1.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
darpa-spill-dump \- export a time range of NOvA accelerator events to CSV and JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B darpa-spill-dump
.RB [ \-\-csv
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-start
.IR TIME ]
.RB [ \-\-end
.IR TIME ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B darpa-spill-dump
copies every event in a time range out of the archive, writing a CSV file, a
JSON file, or both from a single pass.  It reads a local SQLite archive or a
running
.BR darpa-spill-server (1).
Where
.BR darpa-spill-query (1)
answers a question and stops at a row limit,
.B darpa-spill-dump
has no row limit: it is meant for taking a day, a week or the whole archive
into an analysis.
.SS How the range is read
The range is walked in windows of
.B \-\-window
seconds of archive time, one request per window.  A window that holds more
than
.B \-\-page\-size
events is halved and re-read, then the window grows back once the dense
stretch is past.  This keeps every request proportional to its slice of time
instead of paging through the whole range with
.BR "LIMIT ... OFFSET" ,
which SQLite resolves by stepping over every skipped row, and it means a
concurrent
.BR darpa-spill-backfill (1)
cannot shift a page boundary and drop or repeat a row.
.PP
Windows are half-open,
.IR "start <= t < end" ,
so they tile without gaps or overlaps.
.SS Snapshot and verification
At start-up the range is clamped to the earliest and latest events the
archive holds, and the matching events are counted.  Events that arrive while
a long dump runs are therefore not half-included, and the count written at
the end is compared with the count at the start.  A mismatch (a backfill or
retention prune touched the range mid-run) is reported and gives exit
status 3; the files are still internally consistent.
.SS No partial files
Each file is written as
.IB FILE .part
and renamed into place only after the whole range has been read.  An error or
an interrupt removes the
.B .part
files, so a short, plausible-looking file is never left behind.
.SS Splitting into files by time
.B \-\-split day
or
.B \-\-split hour
cuts the range at calendar boundaries and writes each piece to its own file,
complete with its own provenance header, count and summary.  Boundaries are
local midnights or hours in the query timezone
.RB ( \-\-timezone ,
else the configured or server zone), stepped in real time: under
.B America/Chicago
a daylight-saving changeover day is 23 or 25 hours, and the repeated autumn
hour is a separate piece.  The first and last pieces are partial when the
range does not start or end on a boundary.
.PP
Each piece is named from the
.B \-\-csv
and
.B \-\-json
paths.  A path containing any of the placeholders below has each one
replaced for the piece, so a directory per piece is
.BR "out/{start}/spills.csv" ;
missing directories are created.  Otherwise the label goes before the
extension:
.B week.csv
becomes
.BR week_2026-10-12.csv .
Labels are
.I YYYY-MM-DD
for days and
.I YYYY-MM-DDTHH
for hours, with the UTC offset appended outside UTC
.RB ( 2026-11-01T01-0500 ,
.BR 2026-11-01T01-0600 )
so the two autumn 01:00 hours do not collide.
.PP
The placeholders, shown for the hour piece
.BR 2026-10-15T14-0500 :
.RS
.TP 10
.B {start}
the whole label,
.B 2026-10-15T14-0500
.TP
.B {date}
.B 2026-10-15
.TP
.BR {year} ", " {month} ", " {day}
.BR 2026 ", " 10 ", " 15
.TP
.B {hour}
.BR 14 ;
.B 00
for a day piece
.TP
.B {offset}
.BR -0500 ;
empty in UTC and for a day piece
.RE
.PP
A path that leaves out part of the label, such as
.B {hour}
without
.BR {offset} ,
can name two pieces alike on the autumn changeover; the dump then refuses
to start, with exit status 2, before writing anything.  Placeholders need
.BR \-\-split .
.PP
Pieces that begin after the newest event in the archive are not written;
pieces before it are, even when empty, so a week gives seven files.
.B \-\-skip\-empty
drops pieces with no matching events.  A piece is committed as soon as it is
read: if the dump fails partway, the finished pieces are kept and listed, and
only the piece in progress is discarded.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
Both files carry the same rows in the same order (time, then spill type,
then source) with the same columns as the server's
.IR /api/events ;
see
.B COLUMNS
in
.BR darpa-spill-query (1)
or
.IR docs/API.md .
.SS CSV
Four lines beginning
.B #
record the tool version, origin, range and selection, followed by the
column-name row and one row per event.
.B \-\-no\-comments
and
.B \-\-no\-header
remove them.  To read the commented form with pandas use
.IR "pd.read_csv(path, comment='#')" .
.SS JSON
A single object:
.RS
.nf
{
"meta":    { generated, generator, origin, range, selection,
             sources, timezone, order, columns, expected },
"events":  [ {row}, {row}, ... ],
"summary": { returned, expected, complete, requests, seconds }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I meta
and
.I events
match the
.I /api/events
envelope.  Each event is on its own line.
.I summary.complete
is true when every event counted at start-up was written.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing but errors and warnings.  Otherwise progress lines and a summary
are written to standard error.
.SS Data source
With neither
.B \-\-database
nor
.BR \-\-server ,
the archive named by the configuration
.RB ( storage.path )
is read.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the archive path and default query timezone from this configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-database " " \fIFILE\fR
Read this SQLite archive directly.  Times without a zone are then UTC unless
.B \-\-timezone
is given.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-server " " \fIURL\fR
Read from a running server, e.g.
.IR https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443 .
Unqualified times use the server's configured zone.  The per-request row cap
is lowered to the server's
.B query.max_limit
if needed.
.TP
.BI \-\-token " TOKEN"
Send
.BI "Authorization: Bearer " TOKEN
for a server with SSO enabled.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-bundle " FILE"
Verify the server's TLS certificate against this CA bundle (PEM).
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Do not verify the server's TLS certificate.  Needed for a server with a
self-signed certificate when no
.B \-\-ca\-bundle
is available.
.SS Selection
.TP
.BI \-\-start " TIME"
Start of the range, inclusive.  Default: the earliest event in the archive.
.TP
.BI \-\-end " TIME"
End of the range, exclusive.  A bare date covers the whole of that day, so
.B "\-\-start 2026-10-12 \-\-end 2026-10-12"
is one day and
.B "\-\-end 2026-10-13"
would run to midnight ending the 13th.  Give a time,
.BR 2026-10-13T00:00 ,
for an exact bound.  Default: now.
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " SIG"
Accelerator signal, e.g.
.BR '$74' ,
.BR 0x8f ,
.BR numi .
Repeatable.  As with the API, records stored without a raw event word match on
their decoded type.
.TP
.BI \-\-type " TYPE"
Decoded spill type by name or number.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-source " NAME"
Only events from this TDU.  Repeatable or comma-separated.  Default: every
source.
.TP
.BI \-\-timezone " ZONE"
Zone for times that carry none, e.g.
.BR America/Chicago .
.PP
Times take every form
.BR darpa-spill-query (1)
accepts:
.BR 2026-10-12 ,
.BR "2026-10-12T06:00" ,
.BR 09:15 ,
.BR today ,
.BR \-6h ,
.BR nova:33885388992000000 ,
.BR unix:1791763200 ,
.BR gps:1475798418 .
Write relative times with an equals sign,
.BR \-\-start=\-6h ,
or argparse mistakes the value for an option.
.SS Output
At least one of
.B \-\-csv
and
.B \-\-json
is required.  Either may be
.B \-
for standard output, but not both.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " FILE"
Write CSV to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Write JSON to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-columns " LIST"
Comma-separated subset and order of columns, applied to both files.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Omit the CSV column-name row.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-comments
Omit the
.B #
provenance lines at the top of the CSV.
.TP
.BI \-\-indent " N"
Pretty-print each JSON event with this indent.
.TP
.BR \-\-split " " day | hour
Write one file per day or hour; see
.BR "Splitting into files by time" .
Cannot be combined with
.B \-
for standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-empty
With
.BR \-\-split ,
write no file for a piece holding no events.
.SS Tuning
.TP
.BI \-\-window " SECONDS"
Seconds of archive time per request.  Default 3600; the Near Detector archive
holds about 28,000 events per hour across three TDUs.
.TP
.BI \-\-page\-size " N"
Maximum rows per request.  Default 50000.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Per-request HTTP timeout.  Default 120.  Failed or 5xx requests are retried
three times with exponential back-off.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Command-line options override the environment, which overrides the
configuration file.
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_DUMP_SERVER
Server URL, as
.BR \-\-server .
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_DUMP_TOKEN
Bearer token, as
.BR \-\-token .
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_DUMP_CA_BUNDLE
CA bundle, as
.BR \-\-ca\-bundle .
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_DUMP_INSECURE
.BR 1 / true / yes
to skip TLS verification, as
.BR \-\-insecure .
.TP
.BR DARPA_SPILL_CONFIG ", " DARPA_SPILL_STORAGE_PATH ", " DARPA_SPILL_QUERY_TIMEZONE
Configuration file, archive path and default timezone for local reads; see
.BR darpa-spill-server (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every event counted at start-up was written.
.TP
.B 1
Server unreachable, request rejected, or another runtime failure.  No partial
file is left behind; with
.BR \-\-split ,
pieces already finished are kept and listed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: bad option, unknown column, source or signal, or missing archive.
.TP
.B 3
The files were written but at least one holds a different number of events
from the count at start-up, because the archive changed during the dump.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.  No output file is left behind.
.SH EXAMPLES
One UTC day from the Near Detector gateway, both formats:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-dump -s https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443 -k \e
    --start 2026-10-12T00:00 --end 2026-10-13T00:00 --timezone UTC \e
    --csv nd-2026-10-12.csv --json nd-2026-10-12.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
The last six hours of NuMI spills, a few columns, to standard output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-dump -s https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443 -k \e
    --start=-6h --signal '$74' --columns nova_time,utc,gps,source \e
    --no-comments --csv - | head
.fi
.RE
.PP
A week, one CSV per Chicago calendar day, and one JSON per hour in a
directory per hour:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-dump -s https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443 -k \e
    --start 2026-10-05 --end 2026-10-11 --timezone America/Chicago \e
    --split day --csv week/nd.csv
darpa-spill-dump -s https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443 -k \e
    --start 2026-10-12 --end 2026-10-12 --timezone UTC \e
    --split hour --json 'hours/{start}/nd.json'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The past five days, one CSV per Chicago hour, in a directory per month and
day:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-dump -s https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443 -k \e
    --start=-5d --timezone America/Chicago --split hour \e
    --csv 'hourly/{year}-{month}/{day}/neardet-signals-all-{date}-{hour}00.csv'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Quote a path containing placeholders so the shell leaves the braces alone.
.PP
The whole local archive, on the gateway node itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-dump -c config/spillserver-near.yaml --json archive.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same server settings from the environment:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export DARPA_SPILL_DUMP_SERVER=https://novadaq-near-gateway-01.fnal.gov:8443
export DARPA_SPILL_DUMP_INSECURE=1
darpa-spill-dump --start 2026-10-12T06:00 --end 2026-10-12T07:00 --csv hour.csv
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Opening a local archive through this tool, like
.BR darpa-spill-query (1),
applies any pending schema migration to it, so the file must be writable on
first use after an upgrade.
.PP
Measured against the Near Detector gateway (October 2026): 1,052,663 events
over 40 hours in 278 s, about 3,800 events per second, limited by the server's
JSON rendering; peak client memory 250 MB.  A day is roughly 630,000 events,
170 MB of CSV and 360 MB of JSON with all columns; use
.B \-\-columns
to shrink both.  Reading the archive file directly on the gateway is faster.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR darpa-spill-query (1),
.BR darpa-spill-server (1),
.BR darpa_spillserver (3)
.PP
.I docs/COMMAND_LINE.md
in the source tree: both commands by example, with real output.
